  "title": {
    "en": "Reed and marsh warblers"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Reed, Marsh, Sedge, Great Reed, Blyth's Reed, Paddyfield, Aquatic and Grasshopper Warbler"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Acrocephalus warblers are brown, secretive and hard to see. They live in reeds, willows, nettles and thick grass. By ear they are a lot easier than by eye: each species has a different song, and Marsh and Blyth's Reed Warblers copy other birds as well. The standard trio is Reed, Sedge and Marsh Warbler. After that come the rarities that make birders get up before dawn.\n\nSeason: they all arrive late. Sedge Warblers come first, in mid-April, then Reed Warblers in late April, and Marsh Warblers not until late May or June, when the others are already feeding young. The Great Reed Warbler is a southern bird that overshoots in May. Autumn adds a different set: Aquatic Warblers pass in August, and the eastern Blyth's Reed and Paddyfield Warblers turn up from September.\n\nHabitat helps. Reed Warblers live in reeds, Sedge Warblers in rank vegetation on the edge, Marsh Warblers in tall herbage and nettles by the water, and the Grasshopper Warbler is a bird of rough, damp grass."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "supercilium"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The pale stripe above the eye (\"eyebrow\")."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crown stripe"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale stripe along the middle of the crown."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "primary projection"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The length of the wing tip sticking out past the tertials on a resting bird."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "mimic"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "To copy the sounds of other birds. Marsh Warblers can imitate dozens of species, including African ones."
      }
    }
  ],
